When a Kubernetes Node Goes Down: What Happens Next?

Node failures are inevitable but Kubernetes is designed to recover automatically. When a node becomes unreachable, several things happen behind the scenes to keep your workloads running.

Here’s the flow:
- The node is marked NotReady
- Pods on the node stop responding
- The controller manager reschedules affected Pods
- New Pods are started on healthy nodes
- Pods on the failed node are eventually terminated

If your apps aren’t resilient to node loss, the issue isn’t Kubernetes, it’s your configuration.
